Date: Thu, 1 Jul 93 17:00:15 PDT
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: JOB: Be Me on NASA Bayesian AI Research Team

I'm going back to get my PhD at the end of September.  So we're
looking for someone to take my place on our (5 person) Bayesian
Learning research team, here in the (~50 person ) AI (Artificial
Intelligence) branch here at NASA Ames Research Center.

Our Ames AI branch is one of the most highly regarded non-university
AI labs.  My "Bayesian" group is headed by Peter Cheeseman, an
outspoken ex-physicist with broad interests and well known for his
work at the intersection of AI and Bayesian Statistics.  (Actually, 3
of us are ex-physicists.)

We work on many small research ideas, and a few long term projects.  A
few years ago we developed AutoClass, a rich unsupervised
classification system (using finite mixture models).  Now we are
working mainly on inferring a single sharp image model from many fuzzy
images of the same scene.  We have also worked on inferring DNA
history trees, on heuristics to indicate where the hard problems are
in NP problem spaces, and much more.

Since we're now doing image stuff, an image processing background
might be useful, as would math skills, familiarity with statistics, an
AI background, fluency in Commonlisp, and writing skills.  But more
fundamentally we're looking for someone who knows how to do research,
and who knows how to make models to solve problems.

What does that mean?  Say your fearless project leader arrives one
Monday full of enthusiasm for some new research idea.  Can you
tactfully ask enough to figure out what the hell he is talking about,
and help him pare his universal elixar down to some feasible research
project(s)?  Can you figure out who to call, and what books and
journals to skim or read, to learn the basics and find out if this has
been done before, even if this isn't in one of your fields of
expertize?

So now you've got a smaller research idea.  Can you construct a
statistical model just complex enough to possibly gain the hoped for
results, and do whatever algebra, calculus, or Mathematica fiddling is
needed to get approximate formulas for the inferences you want?  Can
you save this math in a form you can interpret later, and can you
explain this math to your research teammates?

Can you then embody these formulas in working code, only trading
simplicity for speed when the need clearly arises?  Can you stay
honest about investing in tools and generality only when it will
really payoff?  Can you negotiate who's responsible for what modules
and define code interfaces so that you each don't have to delve too
deep in each other's code?

OK, now the code doesn't crash when run, and when run on some data
gives ... well something.  Can you search the results for patterns and
clues to what's happening?  Can you think of things that could be
going wrong and devise and prioritize tests to check those theories?
When such tests are passed, can you help prioritize the huge list of
desired features and project directions everyone has come up with?
Can you stay sane while repeating this cycle a hundred times?

Can you tell when its time to stop and whip up a paper, can you
negotiate who will write what, and can you figure out how to
communicate the essence of your part while merging seemlessly with the
rest of the paper?  Can you compose and give talks?  Can you think of
who might actually want to use this in the real world?  Finally, can
you find space to follow your own ideas and get a name for yourself in
this field while still letting the project leader be in charge?

Well maybe no one can do all this, but I hope you get the idea.
Anyway we're trying to make the usual tradeoff between blasting this
job offer to the winds, and being buried in resumes.  So do forward
this to folks you think might be interested, but do be selective.

Pay is "competitive", given experience, as always, and this is
government work.  The official contact for sending resumes, etc., is:

