Subject: Hargis T-raid response

The following explanation of events about the "T-raid" (as I call it) was
presented to account holders by Anthony Hargis. Anthony agreed to let me
e-mail his remarks to internet and he does not object to its further
distribution off the net.

Anthony's attitude still seems to be that "we aren't doing anything wrong, so
we don't need privacy." Sigh.
=====================================================

Anthony L. Hargis & Co.
A Free Market Contractual Company
17220 Newhope #201
Fountain Valley, California Republic

1993 May 29
To our customers:

On May 18 agents of the U.S. Postal Service and U.S. Treasury executed a
search and seizure of our premises, which resulted in the confiscation of
records, 8400 in cash, nine Au coins and three computers, among other things.

The search and seizure was triggered by an event in Florida in early January
involving one of our customers. The customer, a woman, was stopped by p
olice and in excess of forty thousand 'dollars' in cash and postal money
orders (PMOS) was found on her, which was confiscated. The woman had just
learned that she had become pregnant which had created a strain -- or worse
-- in her relationship with her boyfriend.

In this condition of heightene
d emotional turmoil, she intended to 'escape' to a secluded place where she
could make a
 decision to abort or not. In attempting this 'escape' -- wearing a wig,
sunglasses, possessing 
a driver's license of, and representing herself as, her sister -- she was
stopped by the police. And, the above mentioned cash and PMOS were in her
possession.

The situation, to say the least, looked suspicious: She was arrested and the
cash and PMOS were confiscated.

In an attempt to recover her cash and PMOS, she explained that she had
Anthony L. Hargis & Company send the PMOS to her, of which, she had converted
some into cash.

We subsequently began receiving inquiries from postal inspectors regarding
our side of the story. We returned their phone calls, sent copies of the
money orders and provided an affidavit regarding the matter. They asked for
information: we gave it. We had nothing to hide: we were trying to help a
woman recover her property. We explained that the customer had requested us
to send forty five
 thousand in PMOS to her. We told her that we had to buy PMOS with cash and
that we didn't have that much cash lying around -- and, if we complied with
this request it might take 15-20 days. We, accordingly, tried to persuade her
to allow us to send an ordinary bank check to her. She insisted on the PMOS
and that payee and payer spaces be left blank. I explained the request for
blank spaces to be a bad idea also: A money order -- with either space blank
-- can be negotiated by anyone. She, nonetheless, i
nsisted on the blank spaces. So, according to the motto 'the customer is
always right'
 but with very uncomfortable feelings, I proceeded to comply with her wishes:
after all, the property in question was hers.

Now, we had a problem: If we walk into a post office with forty five thousand
in cash, every employee in the building would know about it in 15 minutes. By
the end of the day, all thei
r friends would know of the event. By the end of the next day, half the town
would know. There'
s no way to tell how many cash hungry, dope starved addicts would be lurking
around our door and following us -- looking for an opportunity to kill us for
two bucks.

Since we knew the police weren'
t interested in protecting us, we decided to take the law into our own hands
and protect ourselves. Thus, we decided to purchase 3 or 4 PMOS (700 bucks
each) at a time at different post offices, on different days and with
different clerks. This way, we thought we would give no one any cause to
think we handled cash.

It was this effort to protect ourselves from thieves and dopers that
engendered the wrath of the U.S. Treasury and Postal authorities, resulting
in the search and seizure executed May 18.

The raid was based on the allegation that we are engaged in so-called cash
laundering -- despite our long-standing policy of discouraging the use of
cash. See, for example, the essay \Pathological Investing\, published in
1990; or the essay \On Golden Thoughts\ (1982); or the book \In Gold I Trust\
(1974). It is well documented: we have been \discouraging\ the use of cash
for \twenty\ years.

The raid began at 2 p.m. and lasted five hours. I stayed in the office the
whole time and took several opportunities to practice lecturing 4 or 5 of the
raiders as they expressed interest.

Shortly after the raiding party left, Jane, my former wife, and Aurelia, our
5 1/2 year old daughter, walked into the office. Jane had been telling
Aurelia that government people were in my office taking things away. As soon
as Jane and Aurelia walked in the door, I started walking toward them and
Aurelia ran to me, stuck her head between my knees and clung to my legs,
chattering and giggling: I was doing one of her favorite things: tickling
her.

Aurelia nev
er does that.

Whenever it's my turn to keep Aurelia, it always takes 30 or 40 minutes and
about 6 books to go from grumpf to normal communication. I may be flattering
myself, but, perhaps Aurelia was relieved to learn that those bad people had
not taken me away. After clinging to me for 10-15 seconds, she broke away and
ran to the bookshelf in a back room where I store her books, dolls, crayons,
puzzlaes, rocks and leaves. She quickly returned to the front clutching her
\Aladdin\ book, exclaiming, 
"They didn't take it! They didn't take it!"

I don't mean to imply that Aurelia likes me better than her \Aladdin\ book: I
was just first in her path.

So much for the invasion.

Now we have to consider the possible consequences.

I have no idea what the invaders will do next. I am told they may be
conducting surveillance on us and that they may visit our customers.

Such a possibility shou
ld be a strong reason to investigate and correct one's status as a state
citizen. Now, since we have been invaded despite my state citizenship, why
would I still recommend it? The law supporting sovereign status is
substantial, but has been largely erased from the memory of American society.
Hardly any bureaucrats are aware of it: They just march over it as if state
citizenship were pure nonsense. However, when we go to court and assert the
right issues, judges send those bureaucrats into reverse. We are obtaining
victories at an accelerating rate: I have court transcripts and live
individuals to prove it.


One thing we've learned is that it is much easier to assert one's rights if
one has sovereign status \prior\ to a traffic stop -- which is becoming a
non-event for state citizens.

If you
 want to pursue the topic, I suggest:
Status of Freedom   4 FRU or .32 gm/AU
King's Prerogative   4 FRU or .32 gm/AU
Revolution ii   6 FRU or .48 gm/AU
Citizenship Notes   5 FRU or .40 gm/AU

(Add 2.50 FRU or .20 gm/AU shipping with each order.)

The collection of Citizenship Notes includes explanations from
 the Internal Revenue Code of "wages", "employee", "employer", "Amer
ican employer" and "United States". It is surprising to learn how limited are
the meanings of these words and phrases.

Regarding our legal reaction to the invasion, I am working with experienced
and dedicated people on developing a response. I would like very much to tell
our strategy, but I think i
t unwise to reveal our strategy too soon. As soon as we have a law suit
filed, we'll make copies available.

We are also receiving offers of help from people previously unknown to us: a
lawyer has offered to help, a local TV station has expressed interest, a
newsletter publisher has offered to organize a legal defense fund - to list a
few of the offers.

We are all dedicated to making our situation whole in this matter - even if
it takes five years, ten years, or fifteen years. An instructive example for
us is the IRS raid of National Commodity and Barter Exchange Association
(NCBA) several years ago. Records, computers, gold etc, were taken in that
raid. NCBA sued to recover and won their case. The judge wrote a scathing
opinion against the IRS. The IRS threatened West Publishing with an audit if
the opinion was published. The judge wrote another scathing opinion ordering
the publication of his opinions. NCBA won its case using issues that, in my
opinion, are less fundamental or less consistent than what we will be using.

Let me explain the situation here:

We are dealing with a woman who has surpassed her breaking point and needs
help; not destruction;

We were trying to help a woman recover her property only to be legally raped
by her violator,

We are dealing with a situation where, for twenty years, we have discou
raged the use of cash while our "protectors" accuse us of the opposite;

We have a situation where we tried to protect ourselves from thieves and
dopers -- only to invoke the wrath of our "
protectors" and

We have a child who, probably, will never leave anything of value with her
father for as long as she lives.

I think I can, with some justification, assert that we are dealing with an
agency beyond redemption.

Despite all of the above, I want to acknowledge that every customer will have
to decide what he or she must do to satisfy the need for safety. In making
this decision, I ask that consideration be given to the necessity of
contesting the present matter. If you wish to help us in this matter, the
most effective help you can render is to let us assist you in correcting your
status as a state citizen.

Sincerely,

Anthony L. Hargis

