Date: Thu, 1 Jul 93 21:43:47 -0700
From: dasher@netcom.com (D. Anton Sherwood)
Subject: the Go tree

David Burns:
> As in fencing, I would speculate that every Go strategy has its weakness,
> which can be exploited by a strategy that also has a weakness, etc. Go has
> no stable mountaintop, no equilibrium. (I think.)

At the risk of repeating the obvious (as Penny Priddy said):
That's true only in a finite universe, small enough that a notation of
the entire decision tree cannot exist.  Because Go is deterministic and
finite, a minimax search of the tree is optimal in some sense.

If at least one player is bounded, though, strategy becomes probabilistic
-- by weighting the branches of the tree according to the likelihood of
the other player's taking them, even an unbounded player can benefit, getting
a chance of a big win (instead of a certain narrow win) at the cost of risk.

Story idea:  We colonize a basement universe with VNMs for the sole purpose
of building a computer big enough to compute the "perfect" game.  It reports
back only the sequence of stones in _that_ game, telling us nothing of the
tree; and then destroys itself.  We have lost little of the fun, as any
departure from the perfect game still leads into a vast unknown game tree. 
And the perfect game can be reverse-engineered forever without giving up all
of its secrets!

