Subject: Kim Stanley Robinson: Red Mars

I have now read three of the five novels nominated this year for the Hugo award:
Vernor Vinge, A FIRE UPON THE DEEP, about which enough has been said;
Margaret (?) McHugh, CHINA MOUNTAIN ZHANG, worth reading but irrelevant here;
Kim Stanley Robinson, RED MARS, about which I will now say a few words.

RED MARS is the story of the first twenty or so Mars-years of human
settlement.  (I heard somewhere that it is to be followed by GREEN MARS
and BLUE MARS.)  Though long (>5E2 pages), it rolls right along, with many
strong and credible characters.

A rich background -- the beginning of terraforming, with opposition and
hazards that you might not think of -- is sometimes severely marred by
a communitarian sensibility ("How dare you leave? The community needs you!")
and newage quasi-Marxist economics.

I'll vote for it ahead of ZHANG, but I'll hold my nose as I do.

