Subject: AIT VirtSem: AIT & Physics

Michael Price:

> Since computation power rises exponentially _without limit_ if we can
> create basement universes we'd number-crunch anything by waiting a
> reasonable time.

This relates to a few questions/ideas I have about 
relationships between AIT and physics:

* How few bits does it take to describe the state of the
universe at the Big Bang?  (Or as far back as we can go).

* How few bits does it take to describe the state of the
universe at time T?  In other words, what is the function
"f" in states(T) = f (states(T0))?

* Is T>T0 for all sufficiently large T?  (eg nanoseconds)
Did the universe perform some "computation" between time T0 and 
time T?

Going to other apsects of physics:

* When "information" is destroyed in an irreversible computation,
which definition of "information" are we talking about?
Traditional information-theory?  AIT?  Some other definition?

* Hypothesis: within reasonable time/memory bounds, evolutionary
algorithms (GA,GP,artificial & natural selection, etc.) are
highly irreversible.  (Intuitively, Darwinian selection proceeds
by generating and destroying large amounts of information).

* What is the time/memory cost of implementing a psuedorandom 
number generator as a reversible computation?

* How does reversibility relate to logical depth?  Can 
an irreversible computation produce structures that are
"deeper" than reversibly-generated structures given a finite
amount of time and memory?

* Hypothesis: Physical entropy is an anti-computation.  A 
dissipative structure performs computation within itself at the 
cost of performing a greater anti-computation on the universe.

And three philosophical definitions to ponder:

* A definition of purely individualist "extropy": the state of 
maximing computation within oneself.

* A definition of reciprocal altruist extropy: the state of 
maximizing new computation within oneself by sharing the compressed 
results of logically deep computations, instead of recomputing them from 
scratch.  Here we must account for any entropic (anti-computation) 
costs of communication.

* A definition of memetic extropy: those memes which maximize
new computations in their hosts are most "extropic".  

